  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1117 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nFriday through Saturday, guidance is in general agreement on a wave \ndeepening over the Rockies. As this deepens, southwesterly flow will \nincrease quite a bit over the region, especially in the low levels. \nWarm advection will increase through this timeframe, which will \nresult in a continuation of above normal temperatures. Guidance is \nfavoring much of the area reaching the mid 60s Friday and Saturday, \nwhere some upper 60s will be possible in our south. As a reference, \nour normal temperatures during this time of the year are in the low-\nmid 40s. Moisture will also be on the rise through this timeframe, \nlargely resulting in an increase in cloud cover through the day on \nSaturday. This may limit some mixing potential on Saturday, but we \nare expecting at least seasonal breeziness. \n\nThis unseasonably mild stretch of weather will come to a close late \nthis weekend, with a seasonally strong shortwave trof forecast to \npass through Saturday night through Sunday. A cold front will \ncrash through the area with this, which will be a focal point \nfor our next chance for precipitation (50-80% chances). This \nwill be a quick-hitting system, which should limit overall \nprecipitation amounts. Currently, WPC is highlighting the \npotential for upwards to 0.25\" of rain with this frontal \npassage. Some areas may see upwards to 0.50\", but confidence is \nlower on this. Precipitation chances will move east of the area \nby the afternoon hours for most, with high pressure moving in \nbehind this. This high pressure will settle over the region \nthrough the beginning of the work week, bringing near to \npossibly below normal temperatures to the area. This will feel \nquite different than the weather we have been seeing, so be \nprepared for the cooler weather.",
